    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today with a high-ceiling core, led by our premier speed-power assets. **Corbin Carroll** (79.02) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) stand as our strongest plays; **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a massive park factor and a favorable matchup against Holmes, while **Tyler Soderstrom** draws an elite 16% park boost against Baz, making both near-mandatory starts. **Josh Naylor** (71.44) rounds out the top tier, capitalizing on an opportunistic matchup against Kay, aided by a wind-out advantage that inflates his base projection. We are also banking on **Mickey Moniak** (66.49) and **James Wood** (63.92) to anchor the outfield; despite the risk of elite catcher pop times and hold percentages, their aggressive batting order positioning\u2014#2 and #1, respectively\u2014provides the necessary volume to justify their spots.\n\nManaging the marginal tier requires strict discipline, specifically regarding **Dalton Rushing** (58.70) and **Jose Caballero** (56.90). While their projected scores are serviceable, both remain in \"Lineup Pending\" status; if **Jose Caballero** shifts lower than his assumed #7 spot, his efficiency will dip further into the sub-50 danger zone. Our most difficult bench decision involved **Zach McKinstry** (48.35). He was the clear next-man-up, but the volatility of facing Wacha, coupled with a bottom-third batting order, pushed him below our 50-point threshold for active starters. We have also opted to keep **Austin Riley** on the bench; while his pedigree is unquestionable, the matchup against the difficult Snell and his current slotting in the bottom of the order made him a lower-efficiency play compared to the high-leverage spots occupied by **Seiya Suzuki** and **Brice Turang**. We are actively monitoring the weather reports, as the wind-out conditions benefiting **Cole Young** and **Josh Naylor** could shift throughout the day, potentially altering our exposure to those specific contests.",
